Subject: chrome: compose the device profile at a fixed point in browser startup

Composition ran lazily, on the profile loader's first use. That made the
identity a function of whichever subsystem happened to ask for a profile field
first, which is not a decision anybody made, and patch 0081 claimed an ordering
guarantee it did not have: seed resolution "must be called exactly once, in the
browser process, before any child is launched", enforced by the entry point.
The process half was enforced. The ordering half was not enforced by anything.

Two consequences, both real rather than theoretical.

On Linux, content forks the zygote from ContentMainRunnerImpl::Initialize,
which runs after PreSandboxStartup and before PostEarlyInitialization. The
zygote's command line is built by LaunchZygoteHelper, which calls
AppendExtraCommandLineSwitches, which is where patch 0005 copies
--apostate-profile from the browser's command line. A profile installed later
than the fork never reaches the zygote at all. Worse, Profile::Get caches its
answer in a function-local static: a zygote that reads a profile field before
forking caches null, and every renderer forked from it inherits that null and
reports the host's values. navigator.hardwareConcurrency comes from
SysInfo::NumberOfProcessors, which consults the profile, so this is a total
loss of spoofing on the platform this product is most often deployed on.

On every platform, the browser process sizes its own pools from SysInfo. A
profile installed after that point makes the browser and its renderers disagree
about the same machine, which is the same class of contradiction patch 0005
exists to prevent: the Device-Memory request header a server receives against
the navigator.deviceMemory JavaScript reads.

So the call goes here, at the end of the user-data-dir block in
ChromeMainDelegate::PreSandboxStartup, in the browser process only. This is the
earliest point that satisfies both conditions the compositor needs.
InitializeUserDataDir has just run, so chrome::DIR_USER_DATA is resolved.
Reading the user-data-dir switch instead would not do: on POSIX
InitializeUserDataDir appends that switch back onto the command line only when
the requested directory was invalid, so on an ordinary launch it is absent and
base/ cannot compute Chrome's default, which is branding-dependent and lives in
chrome/common/chrome_paths_*.cc. And no child process of any type exists yet:
content calls PreSandboxStartup before SandboxInitialized, before the zygote
fork, and long before BrowserMainLoop creates a RenderProcessHost.
BasicStartupComplete is earlier still, but the user data directory is not
resolved there, and the directory changes which optional font packs are offered
and therefore which profile a seed produces.

The ordering is now a property of the code rather than a comment. The switch is
on the browser's command line before any child command line is built, so there
is no process type to forget, and Profile::Get's first call parses a switch
that is already there rather than deciding anything.

Nothing here is page-visible. The only observable change is that the identity a
page reads is now the same one on every platform and in every process, which is
strictly fewer contradictions than before. This adds no switch of its own.
Passing --fingerprint=host still composes nothing, and a second instance that
loses the process singleton has still written nothing to disk, because seed
resolution no longer touches the filesystem at all.

--- a/chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc
+++ b/chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <string>
 #include <variant>
 
+#include "base/apostate/compose.h"
 #include "base/base_paths.h"
 #include "base/check.h"
 #include "base/command_line.h"
@@ -1376,6 +1377,39 @@
     InitializeUserDataDir(base::CommandLine::ForCurrentProcess());
   }
 
+  // Apostate: decide this launch's device profile here, in the browser, and
+  // put it on the command line before anything can copy that command line.
+  //
+  // The compositor used to run lazily, from the profile loader's first use.
+  // That made the identity a function of whichever subsystem happened to ask
+  // for a profile field first, which is not a decision anyone made. Two
+  // consequences were real rather than theoretical. On Linux, content forks
+  // the zygote from ContentMainRunnerImpl::Initialize, after this function and
+  // before PostEarlyInitialization; the zygote's command line is built by
+  // AppendExtraCommandLineSwitches, so a profile installed later than this
+  // never reaches it, and the zygote caching a null profile before forking
+  // hands every renderer the host's values. And on every platform the browser
+  // sizes pools from SysInfo, which consults the profile, so a profile
+  // installed after that point makes the browser and the renderer disagree
+  // about the same machine.
+  //
+  // This is the earliest point that satisfies both conditions the compositor
+  // needs. InitializeUserDataDir just ran, so chrome::DIR_USER_DATA is
+  // resolved -- which the command-line switch alone does not give us, because
+  // on POSIX that switch is appended back only when the requested directory
+  // was invalid. And no child process of any type exists yet: content calls
+  // PreSandboxStartup before SandboxInitialized, before the zygote fork, and
+  // long before BrowserMainLoop. BasicStartupComplete is earlier still, but
+  // the user data directory is not resolved there.
+  //
+  // Only the browser composes. A child carries --type and receives the
+  // profile it was given.
+  if (process_type.empty()) {
+    base::FilePath user_data_dir;
+    base::PathService::Get(chrome::DIR_USER_DATA, &user_data_dir);
+    base::apostate::InstallComposedProfile(user_data_dir);
+  }
+
   // Register component_updater PathProvider after DIR_USER_DATA overridden by
   // command line flags. Maybe move the chrome PathProvider down here also?
   component_updater::RegisterPathProvider(chrome::DIR_COMPONENTS,
